Content blocker: the ruleset engine, filter lists and forced activation.

NOT YET IN patches/series. This is the engine and it compiles, but nothing
creates the service and none of the new files are in a BUILD.gn target, so
applying it today changes no behaviour. It is registered once the wiring
below lands, so that the first build to carry it is a build that works.

What this uses, and what it does not write

  Chromium 152 already contains a complete content blocker. components/
  subresource_filter blocks subresources before the request leaves the
  browser, memory-maps a flatbuffer ruleset shared by every renderer, and
  since 152 applies cosmetic rules through style_rule_matcher. components/
  subresource_filter/tools/rule_parser describes itself, verbatim, as "A
  parser of EasyList rules". Both are plain source_set/static_library
  targets with no testonly marker, so both link into production code.

  What upstream has no notion of is a filter list somebody chose. That is
  the entire gap this fills:

    list text -> RuleParser -> proto::UrlRule / proto::StyleRule
              -> UnindexedRulesetWriter -> one merged unindexed file
              -> RulesetService::IndexAndStoreAndPublishRulesetIfNeeded()

  and the existing machinery indexes it, verifies its checksum, versions it
  on disk and publishes it to every renderer. No matching code is ours. The
  fast, heavily fuzzed part stays Google's; the part with an opinion about
  which lists to carry is Aerium's.

Chrome's RulesetService, not a second one

  RulesetConfig allows several ruleset services to coexist, and the first
  design here did exactly that. It was wrong. subresource_filter's renderer
  half is singular - one UnverifiedRulesetDealer per render process, fed by
  one publisher - and 152 ships no second ruleset anywhere in the tree, so
  a parallel publish path would be ours to maintain against upstream for
  ever.

  Reusing the existing service costs nothing: that slot holds Safe
  Browsing's ad filter upstream, and ungoogled-chromium removes Safe
  Browsing, so in an Aerium build it is never populated. This fills an
  empty slot rather than displacing a working feature.

Activation

  Upstream, the filter runs only where a Safe Browsing verdict says so.
  Aerium blocks everywhere, which is a different question, so the answer
  cannot come from that lookup - and cannot come from a pref either, since
  the throttle is in components/ and the pref is chrome/'s local_state. A
  process-global set by the service bridges that, read and written on the
  UI thread only. The throttle then takes the same route DevTools already
  uses to force activation - set the level, record FORCED_ACTIVATION - so
  metrics and the disabled_reason bookkeeping see a state they understand.
  Placed before the DevTools check so debugging still has the last word.

Lists

  EasyList, EasyPrivacy and AdGuard Base ship enabled; users add their own
  by https URL. Plain http is refused: a filter list rewritten in flight is
  an instruction to the browser about what to block, and through allowlist
  rules, what to stop blocking.

  All enabled lists merge into one ruleset, because the indexed ruleset is
  one flatbuffer and matching walks one index - a fourth list costs a
  re-index, not a fourth lookup per request. The content version is a hash
  over each enabled list's URL and content, so RulesetService skips the
  whole cycle when nothing changed, and a refresh that returns identical
  bytes costs nothing.

  Fetches carry no cookies, no credentials and no referrer. A list fetched
  on a daily schedule would be a reliable beacon if it carried identity.

  A list is rebuilt from cached text at startup before any network access,
  so a launch with no connection blocks with last week's lists rather than
  with nothing. A failed fetch leaves the previous copy in use and says so
  on the row; a fetch that yields zero usable rules reports that rather
  than showing "0 rules", which is indistinguishable from a list with
  nothing left to block.

Parsing untrusted text in the browser process

  Deliberate and bounded. RuleParser is pure string handling, allocates no
  attacker-sized buffers, and Chromium fuzzes it continuously
  (tools/rule_parser/rule_parser_fuzzer.cc). It is still privileged code
  reading remote input, so the parse is confined to one free function with
  a plain in/out contract: moving it behind a mojo interface later changes
  that function's body, not the design around it.

Still needed before this is registered in the series

  - BUILD.gn: the new files in a target, and a dep on rule_parser
  - RegisterLocalStatePrefs() called from browser_prefs.cc
  - Something to own an AeriumBlockerService instance
  - The Settings page: list toggles, add and remove, Update now, counts
